Design Choices (Rules):
- If an Interactable either transports the player to another room or shows the player a puzzle to solve, then its first
    interaction must NOT transport the player to another room or open a puzzle. Once it has been interacted with, then
    it becomes a portal or a puzzle. Basically we have to 'unlock' the Interactable first. The successMessage (if there
    is one) will only be displayed in the first interaction.
- If an Interactable has already been interacted with, then further interactions will not require a specific item.
- If an Interactable gives an Item to the User, its successMessage should be "You have obtained a(n) __ ." so that in
    repeat interactions, the string " already." can replace the period in the successMessage to be the new message.
    That Interactable must give the Item in the first interaction, and not again in repeated interactions.
- An Interactable cannot have both unlockedRoomId and linkedPuzzleId non-null at the same time. In other words those
    two are mutually exclusive.

Special Consideration:
- If an Interactable has multiple states (requires multiple interactions, excludes the state where the object becomes
    a portal or a puzzle), then an idea is to make multiple disappearing Interactable objects to represent those states,
    and make it so that interacting with one will make it disappear to reveal the next state. This is done by writing a
    special case in InteractInteractor.java.
- If an Interactable spawns another Interactable or changes the room (e.g. lighting), write a special case!
- If an Interactable does not follow the above design choices then write a special case!

Notes:
- Make sure to create the unlocked Room (if not already done at the very start), linked Puzzle, and reward Item
    entities at the same time (at latest) as the creation of the corresponding Interactable! This will avoid the
    NullPointerException.